type: decision
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/the-backend-runs-on-a-physical-machine-i
created_at: 2026-09-08T09:36:59.279270+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-08T09:36:59.279270+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:8a6a1300-bb21-4ae1-8d79-4957f245fc99] @ 2026-09-08T09:36:59.279270+00:00

The backend runs on a physical machine inside the home LAN rather than on the cloud VPS used for other projects, so nothing needs to be exposed externally - but HTTPS inside a LAN is exactly what old Safari is worst at (mixed content, self-signed certificates), which makes the reverse-proxy/TLS question a deployment-phase risk rather than a settled one.
---
[node:ac006a24-2763-40e7-8423-2b851793efcf]
type: constraint
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/the-google-refresh-token-and-the-kilo-ga
created_at: 2026-09-08T09:36:59.394279+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-08T09:36:59.394279+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:c3dc31be-39b3-4422-a4f6-41136bfb48aa] @ 2026-09-08T09:36:59.394279+00:00

The Google refresh token and the Kilo Gateway key are server-side only and never reach the frontend or the iPad. Varlock manages .env by schema, so agents see the schema and never the values, and secrets must not leak into GlitchTip payloads alongside errors - which is critical precisely because the AI chat logs every call.
