[node:79662ce5-7b55-458a-b0e9-f0efd406d5a8]
type: constraint
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/theming-is-an-architectural-rule-in-this
created_at: 2026-09-08T09:37:21.559263+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:a1174884-8d33-4260-af5b-0f97264a2ea2] @ 2026-09-08T09:37:21.559263+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-08T09:37:21.559263+00:00

Theming is an architectural rule in this project, not a preference: a component's CSS may reference var(--*) from the generated token sheet and nothing else - no hex literal, no rgb(), no font-family, no px radius, with sizes coming from --space-*, --text-* and --control-h*. Swapping the entire design must be a matter of pointing layout.yaml's theme: at a different file, and a CI grep for hex outside tokens.css is what keeps the contract honest. A second theme exists so the claim is testable rather than aspirational.
---
[node:dffe0843-23d7-4095-99e1-1d39a07d1b2a]
type: constraint
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/amber-is-the-ink-teal-is-the-signal-th
created_at: 2026-09-08T09:37:21.675646+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-08T09:37:21.675646+00:00

Amber is the ink, teal is the signal. The handoff asked for amber as the dominant accent plus teal as the second, which read literally gives two accents and makes amber meaningless - if every glyph is amber then amber cannot also mean look here. So amber is body text, borders and chrome (it is everywhere and therefore carries no urgency) and teal is reserved for state that is good and settled, appearing at most twice per card.
---
[node:9b0e63ee-cd5a-4ba9-ba51-f1e773e399b0]
